# tests/qemu-boot.reboot.expected -- required substrings for the REBOOT: a
# second boot of the disk image a disk boot left behind. Same format as
# tests/qemu-boot.expected. tools/check-boot-log.sh reboot <log> checks this
# file and tests/qemu-boot.reboot.forbidden, AND NOTHING ELSE.
#
# The boot under test (slice 6.4, spec P9): run a disk boot, which builds
# target/disk.img fresh and writes to it; then, before anything rebuilds that
# file, boot it again exactly as it was left --
#
#     MINIXRS_VBLK_IMAGE=target/disk.img cargo run -p minixrs-kernel \
#         --target aarch64-unknown-none --release
#
# MINIXRS_VBLK_IMAGE makes tools/qemu-run.sh attach the named file AS-IS: no
# build, no copy. The machine boots entirely from it (firmware, Limine and the
# kernel come off its ESP), and MFS mounts its root partition, which now holds
# whatever the first boot wrote there.
#
# THIS PAIR ONLY -- the common set (tests/qemu-boot.{expected,forbidden}) and
# the disk pair are deliberately not applied. The write battery was designed
# against a fresh image: it creates files that must not exist, fills a file
# that ships empty, grows a directory that ships exactly full. What it does on
# its own leftovers is not defined by this slice (spec P9, ruling 13), so a
# full check of a used image would be judging behaviour nobody specified. The
# persistence probe runs BEFORE the battery for that reason, and the reboot is
# a short boot: it needs to get only as far as the probe's line.
#
# What it shows: a write made during one boot is on the device in the next --
# writes REACH THE DEVICE and survive the machine. What it does not show:
# anything about the flush. QEMU serves a raw image through the host's page
# cache, so the first boot's writes are in the file whether or not the driver
# flushed them (spec P9). Spec mutation 6 -- drop the flush -- was run and is
# the check of that: `flush ok n=1` went missing on the disk boot and nothing
# moved here, the reboot still printing gen=1 and passing this pair. The flush
# has its own marker, in tests/qemu-boot.disk.expected.

# The proof. init read /etc/boots before writing anything, and it held `1`: the
# image ships `0` (ROOTFS_BOOTS_INITIAL), and the only thing that stores `1` is
# the previous boot's rewrite -- an O_TRUNC open, a one-byte write and a close,
# through VFS, MFS and virtio-blk, into the root partition. The digit is
# formatted from the byte that was read. The ramdisk root could never print
# this: it is rebuilt from the boot archive on every boot.
#
# It is gen=1 exactly, not "anything but 0": the reboot is the SECOND boot of
# the image. A third boot prints gen=2 (run by hand, recorded in the phase
# file), which is what shows the counter counts rather than merely changes.
# Mutation 9 (init skips the rewrite) was run: the disk boot is unchanged and
# the reboot prints `persist gen=0` -- missing here, forbidden beside.
minix.rs init: persist gen=1

# The two lines that make gen=1 a statement about THE DISK: the root the
# counter was read from is the GPT partition of the attached image (part=2,
# first sector 71680, 256 blocks), mounted through `vblk`. Both are
# tests/qemu-boot.disk.expected's, character for character, and that file
# explains every field. On a used image `root ok` says one thing more: the image
# label in the partition's first block and the tail label in its reserved last
# block are still there after a boot's worth of writes, so the first boot wrote
# inside the filesystem. (A reboot that fell back to the ramdisk would fail on
# gen=1 regardless -- the ramdisk always reads 0 -- so these two are not what
# catches the fallback; they are what says where the 1 came from.)
[diag vblk] root ok blocks=256 tail=1 part=2 lba=71680
[diag mfs] bdev.ds ok name=vblk ep=
